vscode: open three files you have to find first, edit seven lines
open: logs/api-2024-11-03.log src/support/format.ts
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
    INC-91  Error rate 4.1% and climbing since the 09:38 release. Three
            endpoints are 500ing with the same stack trace.

The log is in front of you. Read the first four lines and you have the
cause: `formatMoney` does not handle a null amount, and the release
rolled it out to every money field in the API.

`formatAmount` is the old helper. It handles null, it is still exported,
and every affected file already imports it — the rollout was gradual, so
both names are in scope everywhere. The fix is to point every *call* at
`formatAmount` and get out.

Do not rename the definition in format.ts and do not touch the import
lines. `formatMoney` stays in the codebase; making it null-safe is a
ticket for tomorrow, when you are not on a call with three people
listening.

**The budget is 93 keystrokes.** Opening the affected files by hand
and editing seven call sites is about four hundred. You do not know which
files they are yet, and finding out is part of the budget.

@keys
| `:vimgrep /pat/gj src/**/*.ts` | find every call site, in every file |
| `:cfdo %s/old/new/g` | change them all |
| `:wqa` | write everything, including the files vimgrep opened for you |

@hints
You do not know which files are affected, so the first command is the one
that finds out. Everything after that operates on the list rather than on
files you chose.
--
The pattern has to separate calls from everything else with the same
name. A call is the name followed immediately by an open bracket. The
definition has a space and an equals sign after it; the imports have a
comma or a brace.
--
`:vimgrep /formatMoney(/gj src/**/*.ts`, then
`:cfdo %s/formatMoney(/formatAmount(/g`, then `:wqa`.

@solution
`:vimgrep /formatMoney(/gj src/**/*.ts` `:cfdo %s/formatMoney(/formatAmount(/g`
`:wqa`. 81 keystrokes, three commands, and you never opened a file.

**The bracket is the whole pattern.** `formatMoney` matches ten things in
this tree: three imports, one definition, one log line and seven calls.
`formatMoney(` matches exactly the seven you want. Under pressure the
temptation is to rename everything and fix the fallout; one character of
precision in the pattern removes the fallout instead. When you need to
separate a call from a declaration, the open bracket does it, and it
costs nothing.

**The list is also the report.** When somebody asks what you changed, the
answer is the quickfix list: three files, seven call sites, one
substitution. That is a sentence you can say on the call while it is
happening, which is worth more than the thirty seconds the route saved.

**Leave the real fix for tomorrow.** `formatMoney` is still broken and
still exported. Fixing it properly means deciding what a null amount
should render as, which is a product question, and asking product
questions during an incident is how a five-minute rollback becomes a
forty-minute outage. Stop the bleeding with the smallest change that is
obviously correct, then raise the follow-up before you close the
incident — not after, because after is when you forget.

A note on the budget. It is not there to make you type faster. It is
there because the slow route — find the files, open each one, eyeball the
call sites — is also the route where you miss the third file, declare the
incident closed, and get paged again in ten minutes. The fast route here
is fast because it is exhaustive, and those two things are the same
property.

@start src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { formatAmount, formatMoney } from '../../support/format';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

@injectable()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(@inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async order(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);

    return { ...order, totalLabel: formatMoney(order.total) };
  }

  public async orderSummary(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);

    return `Order ${order.id} for ${formatMoney(order.total)}`;
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { formatAmount, formatMoney } from '../../support/format';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

@injectable()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(@inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async order(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);

    return { ...order, totalLabel: formatAmount(order.total) };
  }

  public async orderSummary(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);

    return `Order ${order.id} for ${formatAmount(order.total)}`;
  }
}

@start src/domain/report/order.report.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { formatAmount, formatMoney } from '../../support/format';
import { OrderRepository } from '../order/order.repository';

@injectable()
export class OrderReportProvider {
  constructor(@inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository) {}

  public async dailyTotals(customerId: string) {
    const orders = await this.orderRepository.findByCustomer(customerId);
    const gross = orders.reduce((sum, order) => sum + order.total, 0);
    const refunded = orders.reduce((sum, order) => sum + order.refundedTotal, 0);

    return {
      gross: formatMoney(gross),
      refunded: formatMoney(refunded),
      net: formatMoney(gross - refunded),
    };
  }
}

@expected src/domain/report/order.report.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { formatAmount, formatMoney } from '../../support/format';
import { OrderRepository } from '../order/order.repository';

@injectable()
export class OrderReportProvider {
  constructor(@inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository) {}

  public async dailyTotals(customerId: string) {
    const orders = await this.orderRepository.findByCustomer(customerId);
    const gross = orders.reduce((sum, order) => sum + order.total, 0);
    const refunded = orders.reduce((sum, order) => sum + order.refundedTotal, 0);

    return {
      gross: formatAmount(gross),
      refunded: formatAmount(refunded),
      net: formatAmount(gross - refunded),
    };
  }
}

